        </table>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin:56px 0 18px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Frequently asked questions
    </h2>

    <div style="border-top:2px solid #181818;">
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Should I use vitamin C in the morning or at night?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Either works, as long as you use it daily. The studies above did not compare times of day, so pick the time you will stick to. If you choose the morning, finish with sunscreen.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Can I use vitamin C with niacinamide?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Yes. Deep Hydration, Glow Serum already combines vitamin C and niacinamide in one formula, so you can use both without layering separate products.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Will vitamin C make my skin sting?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Some people find vitamin C stings, particularly on sensitive or dry skin. Patch test before first use, and introduce it on its own so you can tell how your skin responds.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">How long does vitamin C take to work?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Weeks for a fresher-looking tone, and months for texture and older marks. A systematic review noted that long-term use may be needed for noticeable change.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37128827/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>5</sup></a></p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Is vitamin C good for oily or acne-prone skin?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">It can suit them, since its main jobs are antioxidant protection and tone support. If your skin is both oily and tight, a hydrating formula with ceramides keeps the barrier comfortable while you use it.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Can vitamin C replace sunscreen?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">No. Vitamin C helps skin deal with oxidative stress from sun exposure,<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29104718/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>1</sup></a> while sunscreen blocks UV before it reaches the skin. Use both.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        References
    </h2>
